{
  "id": "https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/discrimination",
  "type": "Concept",
  "prefLabel": {
    "en": "discrimination"
  },
  "definition": {
    "en": "A conduct or policy that, in any area of public or private life, treats differently individuals who are situated in the same way without objective and reasonable justification or, conversely, treats similarly individuals who are situated differently, with the purpose or effect of nullifying or curtail the equal recognition, enjoyment, or exercise of one or more human rights and fundamental freedoms. Discrimination may be based on nationality; age; sex; sexual orientation; gender identity and expression; language; religion; cultural identity; political opinions or opinions of any kind; social origin; socioeconomic status; educational level; migrant, refugee, repatriate, stateless or internally displaced status; disability; genetic trait; mental or physical health condition, including infectious-contagious condition and debilitating psychological condition; or any other condition."
  },
  "scopeNote": {
    "en": [
      "In equality and human rights law, the concept of discrimination has evolved from a narrow focus on personal attributes to a more nuanced understanding that recognises how prejudice and disadvantage operate through relationships, perceptions, and social structures. The legal function of anti-discrimination rules is twofold: (1) to protect individuals from being subject to arbitrary or prejudicial distinctions and (2) to promote a society in which structural disparities and historic patterns of marginalisation are actively dismantled. In this sense, non-discrimination operates not only as a negative duty (to refrain from discrimination) but also as a positive obligation (to take measures ensuring effective equality). According to the CRPD, also the denial of reasonable accommodation constitutes discrimination. This principle extends equality into the realm of substantive justice, recognising that identical treatment can perpetuate inequality if it ignores individual differences."
    ]
  },
  "notation": [
    "6.3"
  ],
